d side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that does not vary with $x$ for a fixed value of $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/1497231f27da97063782fa93592b038676810b2d8cc0ad89bb97522b7348a3d5.png)\n\n  \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes touching the line do not vary as we move left and right along the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/0ba4cdd42093e6341f0ec89d56c332b1de4bf6794aeae1f3fd4f77e343bb5f59.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "555ed665-7dd7-5e0b-8c9d-39c0f31376d8" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f1c10e1-0b97-5ee2-9d2d-34c38c61cdc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/28cea5c0c95e4693ea10aedebf1f361737dff629bc961efb085c0a5f726b2d17.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306440} {:answer/id #uuid "5801b1ca-0ab4-5470-8403-07565f0c25f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/b51e287d7e6d20932c70c6a81e565b647230416aa54e25f35546d181e4767689.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306441} {:answer/id #uuid "f87d5185-de44-528b-b481-5359d39bb20d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/70a2d28e1c9e5fc7905c999d40151dc32d6cb36a129009a2d869f4f78e4cd73c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306442} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2864f8-3ec7-5bb5-8502-867814b4d165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/eb596c47096ee36672f95a67be1b736c1478dce6f61ac5d7f950f922bc4a6dae.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306443} {:answer/id #uuid "1a725af9-000f-54b5-a08d-8a0ba7920ae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/a161b298270d5483561400697453451cdb2ed41e4e1390fb41f63780b908d5ef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a725af9-000f-54b5-a08d-8a0ba7920ae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/a161b298270d5483561400697453451cdb2ed41e4e1390fb41f63780b908d5ef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306444} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fc9e534-85f6-5319-94e5-66bcaae05a64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65c21155-0ccb-52d7-9cb7-c682f8d6519d" :question/math-academy-id "q-119303" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y-1?$" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,-2),(1,1)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,-2)}=-2-1=-3, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)}=1-1=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,1)}=1-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/a161b298270d5483561400697453451cdb2ed41e4e1390fb41f63780b908d5ef.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "555ed665-7dd7-5e0b-8c9d-39c0f31376d8" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "864c593f-221d-5e1b-b6d4-ce8677ae0184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/59fa7381ffd5a5f3e44dcd741a0147eeab4e3d78279fbfddf843d95494290db1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306447} {:answer/id #uuid "8b76574b-213f-5444-9c5c-80989cec0658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/6a25c6822a691c7a05d0d0282d40acb4b0a9094ea1bd9fb49f56563d2ce8e470.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306448} {:answer/id #uuid "077c3e37-4007-56eb-99e7-d8aee0eeddec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/2c24767dd41695937b7ee0a13ca400ad107741b099a779bca88727d082c4c12e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306449} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d5a300-89a9-5980-baae-ef56014331e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/bb078d2e632ef5cc29f0e03903a148c8caf0ded552ab9b2604c5b3ecf586d0c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306450} {:answer/id #uuid "cce9a353-ae5d-5bf6-9493-855e7f3ce1a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/16840d29a7ef7361417e0a124e0a541c2853191d2c50bee5b40669b8e87f5e4d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9d5a300-89a9-5980-baae-ef56014331e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/bb078d2e632ef5cc29f0e03903a148c8caf0ded552ab9b2604c5b3ecf586d0c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306450} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfc4c34e-7a4d-521d-b0ec-ec5b79e90c3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84ccc057-bf0c-5cad-9a05-aac69aa2e739" :question/math-academy-id "q-119305" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-1,1),(1,1)$ and $(1,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,1)}=-{1}^{2}=-1, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)}=-{1}^{2}=-1, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,-1)}=-(-1)^{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/bb078d2e632ef5cc29f0e03903a148c8caf0ded552ab9b2604c5b3ecf586d0c4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "555ed665-7dd7-5e0b-8c9d-39c0f31376d8" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46b945d7-fa3e-549e-a457-2f84fb20c6fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/92e312e02d6e90adbf1fbe65c2a77a0a52e8123955c6f21ad28577678445dbfa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306454} {:answer/id #uuid "15805134-f3bc-57c4-9833-158574b4bfc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/16a230cce7b982590ddcbfba2862ca5b2df253d6796632a7c31a76cc3b42785d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306455} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa1de9f-8d34-54d3-b400-53989df04305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/727968555be54b474fcb51b972dc24c50ca7afe972d0cea5bae63f4d4db32888.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306456} {:answer/id #uuid "1d524a99-f6b4-5543-9cb6-cd996f1e96e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/0075556b1e97d2c805490deec07f0efa39257ccfc2d99117c7bcbbfc413a0fbb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306457} {:answer/id #uuid "6c76a135-c828-5360-a0d2-8627561989ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/21828e2eb3d107ea12e90d715de9a3c1350e6fd95f03b321be7ab81a8824b065.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c76a135-c828-5360-a0d2-8627561989ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/21828e2eb3d107ea12e90d715de9a3c1350e6fd95f03b321be7ab81a8824b065.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306458} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bc412d0-1bf7-5853-ab2f-3a881d420d14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f1478d3-68c0-55da-a754-1a29e774dc96" :question/math-academy-id "q-119309" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2y-{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,1),(1,1)$ and $(1,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=2(1)-{1}^{2}=1, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)}=2(1)-{1}^{2}=1, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)}=2(2)-{2}^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/21828e2eb3d107ea12e90d715de9a3c1350e6fd95f03b321be7ab81a8824b065.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1343e4de-56ee-563c-bdc8-f8c0cdc54f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04e20c23-b680-54df-8155-45408c7f96ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306461} {:answer/id #uuid "2794c68c-d4c2-5985-b38c-bb2df7e8afc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306462} {:answer/id #uuid "073f9176-373d-53c1-abe9-54e0a19d1e96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306463} {:answer/id #uuid "542f567d-e68b-5ecf-ab13-3aaa443c087f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306464} {:answer/id #uuid "f573038b-7b03-5250-a324-cfab61187543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f573038b-7b03-5250-a324-cfab61187543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306465} :answer-field/id #uuid "a04f6688-573e-59b4-8ccb-b85a4e68b23d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "302c2eb2-363c-5d38-a827-d5d5950356a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-119324" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $y=2,$ the slope field has a positive slope\n- At every point along the line $y=0,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $y=-2,$ the slope field has a negative slope" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,2)}=-2<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,2)$ is negative for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,2)$ is equal to $-2$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,0)}=-0=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,-2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-2)}=-(-2)=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is equal to $2$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/8fc0b5be5c9147c29410ff7a082316403302f2bce7ec25c665ab7ccc5bf90b0f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9b15835-9882-5758-b5ee-924ba48f7efb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fd19e0b-4abc-5626-ae1a-2baf3a5e71e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-96167-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171326} {:answer/id #uuid "baf3e64c-d109-514d-8e48-7975403e283f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-96167-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171327} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3a7bfd-98cb-530b-9917-f65ad982c625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-96167-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171328} {:answer/id #uuid "3674efb6-581e-5520-b053-11fcf50dd7f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-96167-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171329} {:answer/id #uuid "63b6b3a0-9cf0-5f95-b67f-d06c23d6f01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-96167-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63b6b3a0-9cf0-5f95-b67f-d06c23d6f01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-96167-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171330} :answer-field/id #uuid "8108440e-deab-55f3-8701-ae6640db3081" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b60ad5cb-19eb-5974-b3b8-9f949ad75efb" :question/math-academy-id "q-96167" :question/problem "Which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)?$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with $x$ for a fixed value of $y$ is the following:\n\n  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/b3e3fd451b0ebb2b4eb614dd54f5193be179f8bdcf7d84191004ddb80e39b3f8.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes touching the line vary as we go left and right along the line. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/7715c9573d2a434bb5878ff3900632dae7121bd752bab6a9a7745f69de84f0ce.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1343e4de-56ee-563c-bdc8-f8c0cdc54f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "510ac373-dd16-5f71-bec0-eb88c7be68f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306468} {:answer/id #uuid "3813e739-9351-56d3-8720-39efc9b2c25e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306469} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c33ef3-a72e-5fca-8b88-7981c7183ba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306470} {:answer/id #uuid "fc19ad3e-8ea9-598b-8501-dd6240083d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306471} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a6f56e-b3d3-57ff-8fd6-eeea00962f70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4a6f56e-b3d3-57ff-8fd6-eeea00962f70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306472} :answer-field/id #uuid "401e099b-6c93-5cc5-b46b-ae89e91f9358" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6902a5bc-1eb1-59d0-83e7-00dc0fb1fa7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97288" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y+1$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $y=-2,$ the slope field has a positive slope\n- At every point along the line $y=-1,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $y=1,$ the slope field has a negative slope" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,-2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-2)}=-2+1=-1<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is negative for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is equal to $-1$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-1)}=-1+1=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,1)}=1+1=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is equal to $2$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/caa69f6116eba6462c4fe18f2e535d83db350f0797cf1fcd895ac6df9a4d6fd7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1343e4de-56ee-563c-bdc8-f8c0cdc54f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d85016a-c3df-5e1b-ba4e-a3c946cb9687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306475} {:answer/id #uuid "714c6d42-b007-5534-ba37-1d2f035fa285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306476} {:answer/id #uuid "da8fc36f-99b0-52d7-ba19-aa9b5b2290d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306477} {:answer/id #uuid "266d88fc-c792-5054-820d-c6608780a63e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306478} {:answer/id #uuid "c0019ca1-f707-5838-9ae8-0bcfe61fada9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d85016a-c3df-5e1b-ba4e-a3c946cb9687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306475} :answer-field/id #uuid "e40053a9-3249-5479-b63b-cbf93da09373" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efea2738-a114-5133-8b72-e6cc8c2d82b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-97289" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2y$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $y=-1,$ the slope field has a negative slope\n- At every point along the line $y=0,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $y=1,$ the slope field has a positive slope" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-1)}=2(-1)=-2<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is negative for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is equal to $-2$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,0)}=2(0)=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,1)}=2(1)=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is equal to $2$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/69065d8e064b0fd679f059925f5445dcb2c7d037f180d3a144ee527ea408e8db.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1343e4de-56ee-563c-bdc8-f8c0cdc54f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "439722bb-d102-546e-862c-957d0394576a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306482} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ac134f-8b7c-5847-afc2-9c3ec5012a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306483} {:answer/id #uuid "afb4f72b-27e4-5e75-8e43-f8971796cf5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306484} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f4b3a9-7ad1-52a6-8141-558c25bb5c1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306485} {:answer/id #uuid "91b37e25-54fc-50da-a8aa-0a733deca278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "439722bb-d102-546e-862c-957d0394576a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306482} :answer-field/id #uuid "1367ef06-b74c-5d12-b678-36ebf0cba3cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b75acef-f7be-592d-8f9a-7508b45941e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-97291" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $y=-2,$ the slope field has a negative slope\n- At every point along the line $y=0,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $y=1,$ the slope field has a positive slope" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,-2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-2)}=(-2)^{2}=4>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is equal to $4$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,0)}=(0)^{2}=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,1)}=(1)^{2}=1>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is equal to $1$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/0c6e2e486ae770bc99ef9fe41f02d224de65119ade910d303ad3f57f12e8d84b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1343e4de-56ee-563c-bdc8-f8c0cdc54f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5f99fb6-df4e-5ea2-bd6e-4bc40164a6b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306489} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d7969f-317d-567f-8fbb-37baf68aaca2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306490} {:answer/id #uuid "bb774822-2229-5471-b85e-ed711cecd7cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306491} {:answer/id #uuid "9aff8853-b92a-531c-a0e2-368b68371770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306492} {:answer/id #uuid "71d3c551-ef78-5361-8015-7a58036a0cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5f99fb6-df4e-5ea2-bd6e-4bc40164a6b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306489} :answer-field/id #uuid "733b35b8-eaab-5ef6-85d9-6635605f3f8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a0a3c6e-b7b2-5bdb-b099-81675d5f04cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-97292" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $y=-1,$ the slope of the slope field does not vary\n- At every point along the line $x=1,$ the slope of the slope field varies\n- At every point along the line $y=0,$ the slope field is horizontal" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-1)}=-(-1)^{2}=-1,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is equal to $-1$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,y)}=-{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(1,y)$ depends on $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,0)}=-(0)^{2}=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/272427db87d74e3d4ae09dc69b7add53e8def9ead184a1ab90afc99fc0a7628e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "555ed665-7dd7-5e0b-8c9d-39c0f31376d8" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4905f664-bb57-5a6e-81b8-81866fd15d47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/6c49fd5b31bc2fd864ce11f7bc5153ed9545ab8159ed2a066c0b3e7418edb264.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306496} {:answer/id #uuid "c5048331-ba23-5fc2-8163-6f5a37f07734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/9404a42f9a2c91b7b3032036dc20f0949abec0507e60742e071e385e62b37c43.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306497} {:answer/id #uuid "e2edbe7f-f2be-50bf-bf1f-fae8f2b04d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/e53d6d0e5a2bd49748438755c89c620c1cc9ac52d8f1d4603245f5b20a41ec45.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306498} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d25868-c9f6-5288-80c3-6284d59fa42c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/e98bd7245cbbd5854bd461a8fa1bbb42cef768ee842e71cc3372fa7fed85d804.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306499} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ae8c68-7209-58d9-893f-ace3141a2bd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/6c63fba1f418490eee24c2ce5269fecef7e56a0e86896918e736bb51b2b89f11.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9d25868-c9f6-5288-80c3-6284d59fa42c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/e98bd7245cbbd5854bd461a8fa1bbb42cef768ee842e71cc3372fa7fed85d804.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306499} :answer-field/id #uuid "51b227ee-03da-59b4-b7a3-af6105d6dd46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "976c8088-9254-55d6-a90b-4d77854e8e0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97620" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-3y+2?$" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-1,2),(3,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,2)}=-3(2)+2=-4, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(3,2)}=-3(2)+2=-4, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,-1)}=-3(-1)+2=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/e98bd7245cbbd5854bd461a8fa1bbb42cef768ee842e71cc3372fa7fed85d804.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "555ed665-7dd7-5e0b-8c9d-39c0f31376d8" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0bf6389-7a83-550d-86a4-83f48b1bb6d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-97622-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171458} {:answer/id #uuid "e43868e2-ac80-53c7-9254-d3175c0550a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-97622-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171459} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c119b9-6d02-5deb-819c-543487e56ffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-97622-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171460} {:answer/id #uuid "033f4773-15ea-511a-96b1-2f8ae3e476de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-97622-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171461} {:answer/id #uuid "23f44b99-717a-5dda-ba79-3aa361bec2de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-97622-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5c119b9-6d02-5deb-819c-543487e56ffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-97622-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171460} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8f8f968-b2bd-5bc9-8e29-db075f5ee44b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63ce3b5c-8d44-5ee9-a6b1-5ccb563ad615" :question/math-academy-id "q-97622" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-y?$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,1),(1,1),$ and $(1,-2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=(1)^{2}-(1)=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)}=(1)^{2}-(1)=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,-2)}=(-2)^{2}-(-2)=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/5f2f99159626fad30330210592c4e36859bfc30d7d3cf2c3f9178e1cb93498aa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9b15835-9882-5758-b5ee-924ba48f7efb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4461ae48-d6eb-5d38-a8bb-6837f02b775e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/9f883ac23f1a6574f2afdf2ca4dcb0aaf617c992c6c67f34ca5d3a0af63b8149.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306503} {:answer/id #uuid "dcfda3f4-524b-5053-aaa7-400a8abc357a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/2d6e60649a74a2962207052bcd6e4af075e3c1a2274459b809e9ddd85401234b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306504} {:answer/id #uuid "ec723392-acf6-515d-b678-1cf129120bf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/d479b63dc338f2c94c1e8e0b55e9b0fa62a9f534c4935f264aeed43c926023d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306505} {:answer/id #uuid "08ab3e22-dc8a-5986-8948-3162f057cb24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/c311b0ac3ddea7b67b424adc87fccebef3e5bce445ccb730eb49e41919cd7bab.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306506} {:answer/id #uuid "da047d73-738b-5c0d-9920-1f5bafb1f973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/8fff6034a95ab71a31435a776143acd31316c3441e54bbde5f02a3b867cfb3f3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4461ae48-d6eb-5d38-a8bb-6837f02b775e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/9f883ac23f1a6574f2afdf2ca4dcb0aaf617c992c6c67f34ca5d3a0af63b8149.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306503} :answer-field/id #uuid "e26762b8-cbdd-54d3-b140-288b455d3cba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0de2f442-3f46-5398-9b0c-1c3ef935f719" :question/math-academy-id "q-98018" :question/problem "Which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with $x$ for a fixed value of $y$ is the following:\n\n  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/9f883ac23f1a6574f2afdf2ca4dcb0aaf617c992c6c67f34ca5d3a0af63b8149.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes touching the line vary as we go left and right along the line. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/b157285c42bd83c28ed4b3da51fa6d0e83353ae806781fd4231abd45da880228.png)"}]}
